# A webhook: the payment processor calls this when a contractor's subscription payment clears.
# It's signed with the Standard Webhooks scheme (standardwebhooks.com): webhook-signature is "v1,"
# then the base64 HMAC-SHA256 of "webhook-id.webhook-timestamp.body" with the shared secret.
#
# This exact request is signed correctly, but its timestamp is fixed, so the server rejects it as
# stale (401) once it's more than five minutes old. That's the replay protection working. For a call
# the server accepts, print one with a fresh signature: python3 server/payment_webhooks.py c_8f3a2b
# server/tests/test_example_files.py checks this signature.

@baseUrl = http://localhost:8000

### Payment Succeeded
# Expect: 204 (with a fresh timestamp and signature)
POST {{baseUrl}}/webhooks/payments
Content-Type: application/json
webhook-id: msg_2Lk8xQ
webhook-timestamp: 1794150000
webhook-signature: v1,OsHW/1aAeB+9h9pREs9mMZTyzlb84dc3Vr4XrzAl0cg=

{"type":"subscription.payment_succeeded","data":{"contractorId":"c_8f3a2b","subscriptionId":"sub_4471","amountCents":4900,"currency":"USD"}}

# Response: 204 No Content
# The same webhook-id again also gets 204, but isn't processed twice.
# A wrong or stale signature gets 401, and a payload this endpoint doesn't understand gets 400.
